The Governance Control Plane: A Formal Theory of Adaptive Governance for Evolving Autonomous Intelligence

Stochastic Consolidation with Epistemic Constraints

Ouadi Maakoul

AI : ChatGpt, Gemini, Deepseek, Meta AI , Grok XAi 

Abstract

This dissertation develops the Governance Control Plane (GCP), a supervisory architecture governing the conditions under which authorization is created, delegated, monitored, modified, suspended, and revoked. The central mathematical object is the Governance Gap

\Delta_G(t) = G_{\mathrm{req}}(K_t) - G_{\mathrm{eff}}(X_t),

whose componentwise nonpositivity is equivalent to the non-negativity of the Governability Margin \mathcal{Q}(X_t). The theory proves that governability is the robust viability of the set

\mathcal{S}_\Lambda = \{X : \mathcal{Q}(X) \geq 0 \wedge \Lambda \geq \Lambda_{\min}\},

where \Lambda is an institutional legitimacy variable. This final consolidation extends the deterministic framework into three domains: stochastic viability, epistemic asymmetry, and stochastic multi-jurisdictional compositionality.

The central dynamical equation remains

\dot{\Delta}_G = \underbrace{\frac{\partial G_{\mathrm{req}}}{\partial K}(F_K + R + P)}_{\text{capability pressure}} - \underbrace{\dot{G}_{\mathrm{eff}}}_{\text{governance adaptation}},

where F_K is ordinary capability evolution, R is recursive self-improvement, P is adversarial pressure from competing jurisdictions, and \dot{G}_{\mathrm{eff}} is governance adaptation. The adversarial pressure variable has been renamed from G to P to eliminate overloading with governance effectiveness G_{\mathrm{eff}} and governance requirement G_{\mathrm{req}}.

Three principal contributions are established. First, the deterministic viability kernel is replaced by the stochastic viability kernel, and a clear distinction is drawn between almost-sure invariance — requiring diffusion boundary cancellation \Sigma(X,u)^\top \nabla g_k(X) = \mathbf{0} and second-order drift sub-admissibility — and the weaker notion of mean viability, which permits boundary excursions provided they vanish in expectation. Second, the epistemic information threshold is derived from the posterior Cramér–Rao lower bound as a state-dependent allowable error

\delta_{I,\max}(t) = \frac{\bigl(-\Delta_G(\hat{X}_t)\bigr)_+}{L_{\mathrm{eff}}},

and the theorem is stated as a necessary condition on the estimator, not as a control guarantee. Third, the mean-square network stability condition is corrected: the systemic interaction matrix possesses diagonal entries

W_{ii} = \alpha_i - \tfrac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2 + \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\|,

not the sign-inverted form of the previous draft. The stochastic quarantine corollary is reproved under this correction, yielding the isolation threshold \tfrac{1}{2}\sigma_k^2 \geq \alpha_k + \sum_{j \neq k}\|\mathbf{C}_{kj}\|.

The Master Stochastic GCP Viability Theorem is stated as a sufficient condition under explicit assumptions rather than as an equivalence, since the legitimacy comparison inequalities provide one-sided bounds only. The five-way conceptual separation

\text{Intelligence} \neq \text{Capability} \neq \text{Authority} \neq \text{Governance} \neq \text{Legitimacy}

constitutes the conceptual foundation, and the hierarchy of the theory is

\text{ACP} \to \text{GCP} \to \text{Robust Stochastic Game} \to \text{Multi-Jurisdictional Network}.

The objective is not to stop intelligence. It is to ensure that

\text{governance capacity} \geq \text{governance requirement}

through time, despite self-improvement, competition, uncertainty, stochastic volatility, partial observation, and political constraint. This is the GCP thesis, offered as a formal architecture with proofs, explicit assumptions, and residual risks located outside the cryptographic boundary and inside the human one.

