1. Introduction

1.1 The Second Control Problem

The Agent Control Plane (ACP) establishes the separation

\text{Agent Intelligence} \neq \text{Agent Authority}.

Long-lived autonomous systems create a second problem: an agent with initial capability C_0 and delegated authority A_0 may over time acquire new tools, generate software, and operate in novel environments. Its effective capability evolves,

C_t \to C_{t+1},

even when A_t = A_0. This yields the fundamental governance requirement

C_{t+1} \nRightarrow A_{t+1}.

1.2 The Central Dynamical Object

Rather than treating governability as an instantaneous scalar property, the theory organizes itself around the Governance Gap

\boxed{\Delta_G(t) = G_{\mathrm{req}}(K_t) - G_{\mathrm{eff}}(X_t).}

The system is governable in the technical sense precisely when \Delta_G(t) \leq 0 componentwise. The central dynamical equation of the theory is

\boxed{\dot{\Delta}_G = \underbrace{\frac{\partial G_{\mathrm{req}}}{\partial K}\bigl(F_K + R + P\bigr)}_{\text{capability pressure}} - \underbrace{\dot{G}_{\mathrm{eff}}}_{\text{governance adaptation}},}

where F_K is ordinary capability evolution, R is recursive self-improvement, P is adversarial pressure from competing jurisdictions, and \dot{G}_{\mathrm{eff}} is governance adaptation.

1.3 Contributions of the Present Consolidation

This final theoretical consolidation extends the deterministic framework into three domains:

1. Stochastic viability kernel. The deterministic viability kernel \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}_\Lambda) is replaced by the stochastic viability kernel \mathrm{Viab}_{\mathrm{stoch}}(\mathcal{S}_\Lambda), characterized by the second-order Nagumo condition. We distinguish between almost-sure invariance (requiring diffusion boundary cancellation) and mean viability (allowing balanced boundary excursions).
2. Stochastic Hamilton–Jacobi–Isaacs equation. The deterministic max–min Bellman equation is replaced by the second-order HJI PDE, whose value function is characterized as a viscosity solution.
3. Epistemic information threshold. A critical estimation error bound \delta_{I,\max}(t) is derived from the posterior Cramér–Rao lower bound. The correct statement is a necessary condition on the estimator: governability is impossible when the estimation error exceeds the state-dependent allowable margin, regardless of control magnitude.
4. Corrected mean-square network stability. The M-matrix condition is extended to mean-square exponential stability for stochastic interconnected systems. The corrected diagonal entries W_{ii} = \alpha_i - \tfrac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2 + \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| replace the sign-inverted form of the previous draft.
5. Stochastic quarantine corollary. A compliant jurisdiction can isolate a volatile node k by truncating the coupling \mathbf{C}_{ik} \to \mathbf{0}, provided the remaining interaction matrix is a nonsingular M-matrix.
6. Master theorem as sufficient condition. The Master Stochastic GCP Viability Theorem is stated as a sufficient condition under explicit assumptions rather than as an equivalence.

