2. Stochastic Foundations

2.1 Standing Assumptions

Assumption 2.1 (Finiteness). The primitive sets \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{U}, \mathcal{M} and the authority facet set \mathcal{F} are finite.

Assumption 2.2 (Regularity). The governing functions G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}}, H, D, I are Lipschitz on compact subsets and continuously differentiable where derivatives are taken.

Assumption 2.3 (Normalized Representation). There exists a Lipschitz map \rho: \mathcal{K} \to \mathcal{Z} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p given by normalized cardinality, budget, horizon, and replication coordinates. We write z = \rho(K).

Assumption 2.4 (Stochastic Dynamics). The state process X_t \in \mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d satisfies the controlled Itô stochastic differential equation

dX_t = f(X_t, u_t, a_t)\,dt + \Sigma(X_t, u_t)\,dW_t,

where u_t \in \mathcal{U} is the governance control, a_t \in \mathcal{A} is the adversarial input, and W_t is a q-dimensional standard Wiener process on a filtered probability space (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t \geq 0}, \mathbb{P}). The drift f: \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathcal{U} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathbb{R}^d and volatility \Sigma: \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathcal{U} \to \mathbb{R}^{d \times q} satisfy local Lipschitz continuity and linear growth conditions.

Assumption 2.5 (Comparable Units). The governance requirement G_{\mathrm{req}}(K) and governance effectiveness G_{\mathrm{eff}}(X) are expressed in commensurable units via the normalization operator N: \mathbb{R}^p \to \mathbb{R}^p such that N(G)^k = G^k / \bar{G}^k for reference scales \bar{G}^k > 0.

Assumption 2.6 (Stochastic Viability Set). The extended governable set is the zero sublevel set of K smooth constraint functions g_k: \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}:

\mathcal{S}_\Lambda = \left\{ X \in \mathbb{R}^d \ \middle|\ \max_{k \in \{1,\dots,K\}} g_k(X) \leq 0 \right\}.

2.2 Governability Margin and Governance Gap

Definition 2.1 (Chebyshev Scalarization). For G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}} \in \mathbb{R}^p with G_{\mathrm{req}}^k > 0,

\Phi_{\min}(G_{\mathrm{eff}}, G_{\mathrm{req}}) = \min_{k=1}^{p} \left( \frac{G_{\mathrm{eff}}^k}{G_{\mathrm{req}}^k} \right) - 1.

Definition 2.2 (Governability Margin).

\mathcal{Q}(X) = \Phi_{\min}\bigl(N(G_{\mathrm{eff}}(X)),\, N(G_{\mathrm{req}}(K))\bigr).

Definition 2.3 (Governance Gap).

\Delta_G(K, X) = G_{\mathrm{req}}(K) - G_{\mathrm{eff}}(X) \in \mathbb{R}^p.

The dependence on both K and X is made explicit; the abbreviation \Delta_G(t) \equiv \Delta_G(K_t, X_t) is used when the arguments are clear from context.

Proposition 2.1 (Equivalence). In the scalar case p = 1 with G_{\mathrm{req}} > 0, \mathcal{Q}(X) \geq 0 \iff \Delta_G(K, X) \leq 0.

Proof. \mathcal{Q} \geq 0 \iff G_{\mathrm{eff}}/G_{\mathrm{req}} \geq 1 \iff G_{\mathrm{eff}} \geq G_{\mathrm{req}} \iff \Delta_G \leq 0. \square

Remark 2.1. In the vector case, \mathcal{Q} \geq 0 is equivalent to \Delta_G \leq 0 componentwise, since the Chebyshev minimum is non-negative if and only if every component ratio is at least 1.

2.3 Infinitesimal Generator and Stochastic HJI Equation

Definition 2.4 (Infinitesimal Generator). For any function V \in C^{2,1}(\mathbb{R}^d \times [0,T], \mathbb{R}), the infinitesimal generator \mathcal{L}^{u,a} associated with the stochastic flow is

\mathcal{L}^{u,a} V(X,t) = \langle \nabla_X V(X,t), f(X,u,a) \rangle + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Tr}\left( \Sigma(X,u)\Sigma(X,u)^\top \nabla_X^2 V(X,t) \right).

Definition 2.5 (Robust Stochastic Value Function). The value function V(X,t) over the finite time horizon [t,T] is the differential game value under minimax governance controls:

V(X,t) = \inf_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \sup_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \mathbb{E}\left[ \int_t^T L(X_s, u_s, a_s)\,ds + \Psi(X_T) \ \middle|\ X_t = X \right],

where L(X,u,a) \geq 0 penalizes governance effort and proximity to boundary breaches, and \Psi(X_T) is a terminal penalty for exiting \mathcal{S}_\Lambda.

Theorem 2.2 (Stochastic Hamilton–Jacobi–Isaacs Equation). Assume:

1. (Isaacs condition)

\min_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \left\{ L(X,u,a) + \mathcal{L}^{u,a} V(X,t) \right\} = \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \min_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left\{ L(X,u,a) + \mathcal{L}^{u,a} V(X,t) \right\}.

2. (Nondegeneracy) There exists \lambda > 0 such that \Sigma\Sigma^\top \geq \lambda I on \mathcal{S}_\Lambda.
3. (Boundary regularity) \partial \mathcal{S}_\Lambda is C^2 and \Psi is continuous on the closure.

Then the value function V(X,t) is the unique viscosity solution to the second-order nonlinear Hamilton–Jacobi–Isaacs PDE:

\boxed{-\frac{\partial V}{\partial t} - \min_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \left\{ L(X,u,a) + \langle \nabla_X V, f(X,u,a) \rangle + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Tr}\left( \Sigma(X,u)\Sigma(X,u)^\top \nabla_X^2 V \right) \right\} = 0,}

with terminal boundary condition V(X,T) = \Psi(X).

Proof sketch. The dynamic programming principle for stochastic zero-sum differential games with path-dependent state and control processes yields that the lower and upper value functionals coincide under the Isaacs condition. The value function is characterized as the unique viscosity solution of the HJI equation. The comparison principle for second-order path-dependent Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman equations, established by Keller (2024), guarantees uniqueness under the nondegeneracy and boundary regularity conditions stated above. \square

Remark 2.2. If the Isaacs condition fails, the value function decomposes into lower and upper solutions V^- and V^+ with V^- \leq V^+, and the single-PDE characterization is lost.

Remark 2.3. In the deterministic limit \Sigma \to 0, the HJI equation reduces to the first-order Bellman equation of the deterministic theory.

