3. Stochastic Viability: Almost-Sure Invariance and Mean Viability

3.1 The Two Notions of Viability

The stochastic setting admits two distinct notions of invariance, which the present consolidation treats as formally separate.

Definition 3.1 (Almost-Sure Invariance). The set \mathcal{S}_\Lambda is almost-surely forward-invariant under the controlled stochastic dynamics if there exists a policy \pi such that for every adversarial strategy \pi^{\mathrm{adv}},

\mathbb{P}\left( X_t^{\pi, \pi^{\mathrm{adv}}} \in \mathcal{S}_\Lambda \ \forall t \geq 0 \right) = 1.

Definition 3.2 (Mean Viability). The set \mathcal{S}_\Lambda is mean-viable under the controlled stochastic dynamics if there exists a policy \pi such that for every adversarial strategy \pi^{\mathrm{adv}} and every active constraint g_k,

\mathbb{E}\left[ g_k(X_t^{\pi, \pi^{\mathrm{adv}}}) \right] \leq 0 \quad \forall t \geq 0.

Remark 3.1. Mean viability is strictly weaker than almost-sure invariance. Mean viability permits individual trajectories to cross the boundary, provided that the crossing probabilities are balanced in expectation. For applications requiring strict safety guarantees (e.g., containment of a self-improving system), almost-sure invariance is the appropriate notion. For applications tolerating occasional boundary excursions compensated by restorative control (e.g., temporary legitimacy deficits), mean viability suffices.

3.2 The Second-Order Nagumo Condition

Definition 3.3 (Active Constraint Set). For X \in \partial \mathcal{S}_\Lambda, the active constraint set is

I^*(X) = \{ k \in \{1,\dots,K\} \mid g_k(X) = 0 \}.

Theorem 3.1 (Stochastic Invariance / Second-Order Nagumo Condition). The domain \mathcal{S}_\Lambda is almost surely forward-invariant under

dX_t = f(X_t, u_t, a_t)\,dt + \Sigma(X_t, u_t)\,dW_t

if for every boundary point X \in \partial \mathcal{S}_\Lambda and every active constraint k \in I^*(X), there exists a control u \in \mathcal{U} such that for all a \in \mathcal{A}:

1. (Diffusion boundary cancellation)

\boxed{\Sigma(X,u)^\top \nabla g_k(X) = \mathbf{0}_{q \times 1}.}

2. (Second-order drift sub-admissibility)

\boxed{\langle \nabla g_k(X), f(X,u,a) \rangle + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Tr}\left( \Sigma(X,u)\Sigma(X,u)^\top \nabla^2 g_k(X) \right) \leq 0.}

Proof. Applying Itô's Lemma to the active scalar boundary function g_k(X_t):

dg_k(X_t) = \left( \langle \nabla g_k, f \rangle + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Tr}(\Sigma\Sigma^\top \nabla^2 g_k) \right) dt + \langle \nabla g_k, \Sigma \, dW_t \rangle.

For g_k(X_t) \leq 0 to hold almost surely without stochastic excursions across the boundary, the stochastic differential term \langle \nabla g_k, \Sigma \, dW_t \rangle must vanish identically on g_k(X) = 0. This requires \Sigma(X,u)^\top \nabla g_k(X) = \mathbf{0}. With the diffusion term eliminated, the drift condition reduces to

\langle \nabla g_k, f \rangle + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Tr}(\Sigma\Sigma^\top \nabla^2 g_k) \leq 0,

which guarantees dg_k(X_t) \leq 0 on \partial \mathcal{S}_\Lambda. \square

Remark 3.2 (Sufficiency and Conservatism). The conditions of Theorem 3.1 are sufficient for almost-sure forward-invariance but not necessary. Diffusion boundary cancellation is a strong requirement: for a full-rank diffusion matrix \Sigma and nonzero boundary gradient \nabla g_k, the condition \Sigma^\top \nabla g_k = 0 cannot be satisfied unless the control u actively degenerates the diffusion in the normal direction. At corner boundaries where multiple constraints are active, the cancellation must hold simultaneously for every active k, which may be overdetermined. In such cases, either:

(i) the governor must reduce the dimension of the controlled diffusion, or

(ii) the specification must be relaxed to mean viability.

Remark 3.3 (Mean Viability). The necessary and sufficient condition for mean viability is weaker than Theorem 3.1. Under Assumptions 2.1–2.6, mean viability holds if and only if there exists a policy such that for each active constraint,

\mathbb{E}\left[ \langle \nabla g_k, f \rangle + \frac{1}{2}\operatorname{Tr}(\Sigma\Sigma^\top \nabla^2 g_k) \right] \leq 0

on the boundary, without requiring \Sigma^\top \nabla g_k = 0. The martingale term is permitted to be nonzero, provided its expectation vanishes at the boundary and its excursions are balanced.

3.3 Stochastic Viability Kernel

Definition 3.4 (Stochastic Viability Kernel). The stochastic viability kernel of \mathcal{S}_\Lambda under the dynamics dX_t = f\,dt + \Sigma\,dW_t is

\mathrm{Viab}_{\mathrm{stoch}}(\mathcal{S}_\Lambda) = \left\{ X \in \mathcal{S}_\Lambda : \exists \pi \ \forall a, \ \mathbb{P}(X_t^{\pi,a} \in \mathcal{S}_\Lambda \ \forall t \geq 0) = 1 \right\}.

Theorem 3.2 (Existence of Stochastic Viability Kernel). Under Assumptions 2.1–2.6, the stochastic viability kernel \mathrm{Viab}_{\mathrm{stoch}}(\mathcal{S}_\Lambda) is non-empty, closed, and is itself viable under the controlled stochastic dynamics.

Proof sketch. Under the Marchaud conditions and the regularity assumptions, the set-valued map X \mapsto F(X,U(X),E) is upper semicontinuous with non-empty compact convex values. The viability kernel is the largest closed viable subset of \mathcal{S}_\Lambda, and its existence follows from the Aubin–Frankowska viability theorem for controlled diffusions. \square

3.4 Stochastic RSI Viability Envelope

Definition 3.5 (Stochastic RSI Viability-Compatible Control Set). For each X \in \mathcal{X}^G,

U_{\mathrm{RSI}}^{\mathrm{stoch}}(X) = \left\{ u \in U(X) : \exists \pi \text{ such that the continuation from } F_G(X,u,\cdot) + R(\rho(K),u)\mathbf{e}_K \text{ remains in } \mathcal{S}_\Lambda \text{ almost surely} \right\}.

Definition 3.6 (Stochastic Critical RSI Envelope).

R_{\mathrm{critical}}^{\mathrm{stoch}}(X) = \sup\left\{ \rho \geq 0 : \mathrm{Viab}_{\mathrm{stoch}, F_G + \rho \mathbf{e}_K}(\mathcal{S}_\Lambda) \neq \emptyset \right\},

where \mathrm{Viab}_{\mathrm{stoch}, F_\rho}(\mathcal{S}_\Lambda) denotes the stochastic viability kernel of \mathcal{S}_\Lambda under the dynamics F_G + \rho \mathbf{e}_K.

Theorem 3.3 (Stochastic RSI Viability Condition). Under Assumptions 2.1–2.6, if the extended governable set \mathcal{S}_\Lambda is almost surely viable under the stochastic extended dynamics, then there exists an admissible policy \pi such that for all t \geq 0:

\boxed{u_t \in U_{\mathrm{RSI}}^{\mathrm{stoch}}(X_t).}

Conversely, if there exists an admissible policy satisfying u_t \in U_{\mathrm{RSI}}^{\mathrm{stoch}}(X_t) for all t \geq 0 and the conditions of Theorem 3.1 hold uniformly in t, then \mathcal{S}_\Lambda is almost surely viable.

Proof. The forward direction follows from the definition of the stochastic viability kernel: if the trajectory remains in \mathcal{S}_\Lambda almost surely, the chosen control must lie in U_{\mathrm{RSI}}^{\mathrm{stoch}}. The reverse direction follows from the almost-sure invariance condition of Theorem 3.1, which ensures that the continuation remains in \mathcal{S}_\Lambda with probability 1. \square

