4. Epistemic Asymmetry and Information-Restricted Governance

4.1 Observation Model and Kushner–Stratonovich Filter

Definition 4.1 (Observation Model). The governor observes a noise-corrupted signal Y_t \in \mathbb{R}^m given by

dY_t = h(X_t)\,dt + \Gamma\,dB_t,

where h: \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}^m is the observation function, \Gamma \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times r} is the observation noise intensity, and B_t is an r-dimensional standard Wiener process independent of W_t.

Definition 4.2 (Conditional Distribution). The filtering problem is to compute the conditional distribution \pi_t(dx) = \mathbb{P}(X_t \in dx \mid \mathcal{F}_t^Y), where \mathcal{F}_t^Y = \sigma(Y_s : 0 \leq s \leq t) is the observation filtration.

Theorem 4.1 (Kushner–Stratonovich Equation). Under regularity conditions on f, \Sigma, h, the conditional distribution \pi_t satisfies the Kushner–Stratonovich equation:

d\pi_t(\phi) = \pi_t(\mathcal{L}\phi)\,dt + \pi_t\left(\phi h^\top - \pi_t(\phi)\pi_t(h)^\top\right)(\Gamma\Gamma^\top)^{-1}\left(dY_t - \pi_t(h)\,dt\right),

for all test functions \phi \in C_b^2(\mathbb{R}^d), where \mathcal{L} is the infinitesimal generator of the signal process X_t.

Proof. The result is the standard nonlinear filtering equation for partially observed systems. The derivation proceeds via the reference probability measure approach: the unnormalized filtering process satisfies the Zakai equation, and the Kushner–Stratonovich equation is obtained by normalization. \square

Definition 4.3 (Minimum Mean-Square Error Estimator). The MMSE state estimate is

\hat{X}_t = \mathbb{E}[X_t \mid \mathcal{F}_t^Y] = \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} x \, \pi_t(dx).

4.2 Posterior Cramér–Rao Lower Bound

Definition 4.4 (Fisher Information Matrix). For the filtering problem with observation model dY_t = h(X_t)dt + \Gamma dB_t, the posterior Fisher information matrix is

\mathbf{J}_t = \mathbb{E}\left[ \left( \nabla_X \log p(X_t \mid \mathcal{F}_t^Y) \right) \left( \nabla_X \log p(X_t \mid \mathcal{F}_t^Y) \right)^\top \right].

Theorem 4.2 (Posterior Cramér–Rao Lower Bound). For any unbiased estimator \hat{X}_t of X_t based on \mathcal{F}_t^Y, the mean-square error satisfies

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|\hat{X}_t - X_t\|^2 \right] \geq \operatorname{Tr}\left( \mathbf{J}_t^{-1} \right),

where \mathbf{J}_t is the posterior Fisher information matrix.

Proof. The result follows from the van Trees version of the Cramér–Rao inequality for random parameters. The posterior Fisher information matrix \mathbf{J}_t is the inverse of the Cramér–Rao bound for the mean-square error matrix. \square

Remark 4.1 (Interpretation). The bound is a lower bound on the expected squared error. It does not assert the existence of an almost-sure bound on \delta_I(t) = \|\hat{X}_t - X_t\|, nor does it guarantee that any particular estimator attains the bound. The bound is a fundamental limit of cognizability: no estimator, unbiased or otherwise, can achieve mean-square error below \operatorname{Tr}(\mathbf{J}_t^{-1}).

4.3 State-Dependent Critical Information Threshold

Definition 4.5 (Estimation Error). The estimation error at time t is

\delta_I(t) = \|\hat{X}_t - X_t\|.

Assumption 4.1 (Bounded Estimation Error). There exists a deterministic function \delta_{I,\max}: [0,\infty) \to (0,\infty) such that for all t \geq 0, \delta_I(t) \leq \delta_{I,\max}(t) with probability 1. This is an assumption on the estimator, not a consequence of the CRLB.

Theorem 4.3 (State-Dependent Critical Information Threshold). Suppose the governance control policy \pi is based on the estimate \hat{X}_t rather than the true state X_t. Let L_{\mathrm{eff}} be the Lipschitz constant of G_{\mathrm{eff}}. Define the state-dependent allowable error

\delta_{I,\max}(t) = \frac{\bigl(-\Delta_G(\hat{X}_t)\bigr)_+}{L_{\mathrm{eff}}},

where (\cdot)_+ = \max(\cdot, 0). If \delta_I(t) > \delta_{I,\max}(t), then

\Delta_G(X_t) > 0

for all admissible control policies u \in \mathcal{U}, regardless of the control magnitude \|G_{\mathrm{eff}}\|.

Proof. The governance gap is a function of the true state:

\Delta_G(X_t) = G_{\mathrm{req}}(K_t) - G_{\mathrm{eff}}(X_t).

When the governor uses the estimate \hat{X}_t, the effective governance effectiveness is G_{\mathrm{eff}}(\hat{X}_t). The true governance gap satisfies

\Delta_G(X_t) = \Delta_G(\hat{X}_t) + \left( G_{\mathrm{eff}}(\hat{X}_t) - G_{\mathrm{eff}}(X_t) \right).

The estimation error term satisfies

\|G_{\mathrm{eff}}(\hat{X}_t) - G_{\mathrm{eff}}(X_t)\| \leq L_{\mathrm{eff}} \|\hat{X}_t - X_t\| = L_{\mathrm{eff}} \delta_I(t),

where L_{\mathrm{eff}} is the Lipschitz constant of G_{\mathrm{eff}}. For the true governance gap to remain non-positive, we require

\Delta_G(\hat{X}_t) + L_{\mathrm{eff}} \delta_I(t) \leq 0.

When \Delta_G(\hat{X}_t) < 0, this is equivalent to

\delta_I(t) \leq \frac{-\Delta_G(\hat{X}_t)}{L_{\mathrm{eff}}} = \delta_{I,\max}(t).

When \Delta_G(\hat{X}_t) \geq 0, the allowable error is \delta_{I,\max}(t) = 0, and any positive estimation error causes \Delta_G(X_t) > 0. Therefore, if \delta_I(t) > \delta_{I,\max}(t), the true governance gap satisfies \Delta_G(X_t) > 0 regardless of the control policy. \square

Remark 4.2 (Necessity, Not Sufficiency). The threshold is a necessary condition on the estimator: if the estimation error exceeds \delta_{I,\max}(t), governability is lost. It is not a sufficient condition: even if \delta_I(t) \leq \delta_{I,\max}(t), the control policy must still be chosen to keep the system in \mathcal{S}_\Lambda. The bound is state-dependent and decreases as the system approaches the boundary of the governable set.

Corollary 4.3.1 (Information–Control Tradeoff). The critical information threshold satisfies the lower bound

\delta_{I,\max}(t) \geq \sqrt{\operatorname{Tr}\left( \mathbf{J}_t^{-1} \right)}

only in the following negative sense: if

\delta_{I,\max}(t) < \sqrt{\mathbb{E}\operatorname{Tr}\left( \mathbf{J}_t^{-1} \right)},

then no estimator can guarantee \delta_I(t) \leq \delta_{I,\max}(t) for all t with probability 1. Consequently, the governor must maintain a minimum level of information quality, characterized by the Fisher information, to preserve governability with high probability.

Proof. By Theorem 4.2, the MMSE estimation error satisfies \mathbb{E}[\delta_I^2] \geq \operatorname{Tr}(\mathbf{J}_t^{-1}). If \delta_{I,\max}(t) < \sqrt{\operatorname{Tr}(\mathbf{J}_t^{-1})}, then there exists a positive probability that \delta_I(t) > \delta_{I,\max}(t), and governability is lost. \square

Remark 4.3. The previous draft's Corollary 4.3.1 asserted a positive lower bound on \delta_{I,\max} in terms of the Fisher information. This was an overreach: the CRLB bounds the expected squared error, not the almost-sure error. The corrected statement is a negative result: no estimator can achieve an almost-sure error bound below the square root of the CRLB in expectation.

