5. Stochastic Multi-Jurisdictional Compositionality

5.1 Coupled Stochastic Dynamics

Definition 5.1 (Coupled Stochastic Multi-Jurisdictional System). For M jurisdictions i \in \{1,\dots,M\}, the coupled capability dynamics are

dX_t^i = \left( f_i(X_t^i, u_t^i, a_t^i) + \sum_{j=1}^N \mathbf{C}_{ij} (X_t^j - X_t^i) \right) dt + \Sigma_i(X_t^i, u_t^i)\,dW_t^i,

where \mathbf{C}_{ij} \geq 0 is the cross-border capability diffusion matrix from jurisdiction j to jurisdiction i, and the W_t^i are independent Wiener processes.

Definition 5.2 (Local Governance Gap Lyapunov Function).

V_i(X_i) = \frac{1}{2} \|\Delta_{G,i}(X_i)\|^2.

Assumption 5.1 (Local Dissipativity). There exist \alpha_i > 0 and \sigma_i > 0 such that

\langle \nabla V_i(X_i), f_i(X_i, u_i, a_i) \rangle \leq -\alpha_i V_i(X_i),

\operatorname{Tr}\left( \Sigma_i(X_i, u_i) \Sigma_i(X_i, u_i)^\top \nabla^2 V_i(X_i) \right) \leq \sigma_i^2 V_i(X_i).

Assumption 5.2 (Coupling Bound). There exists a constant L_{\mathbf{C}} > 0 such that for all i, j,

\|\mathbf{C}_{ij}(X_j - X_i)\| \leq \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| \cdot \|X_j - X_i\|,

and the coupling enters the generator of V_i through the bound

\left\langle \nabla V_i, \sum_{j \neq i} \mathbf{C}_{ij}(X_j - X_i) \right\rangle \leq \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| \bigl( V_j - V_i \bigr).

5.2 Corrected Stochastic Interaction Matrix

Definition 5.3 (Corrected Stochastic Interaction Matrix). The systemic interaction matrix \mathbf{W} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N} has entries

\boxed{
W_{ij} = \begin{cases}
\alpha_i - \frac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2 + \sum_{k \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ik}\|, & \text{if } i = j, \\[6pt]
-\|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\|, & \text{if } i \neq j.
\end{cases}
}

Remark 5.1 (Correction to the Previous Draft). The diagonal entry in the previous draft was

W_{ii} = \alpha_i - \frac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2 - \sum_{k \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ik}\|,

which subtracts the coupling twice. The correct form, derived from the inequality in Assumption 5.2, adds the coupling terms to the diagonal. The coupling contributes +\sum_{k \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ik}\| to the dissipation of V_i, because the coupling removes V_i from jurisdiction i and redistributes it to neighbors. The previous form could make W_{ii} negative even when the coupling is stabilizing.

Remark 5.2. The off-diagonal entries are non-positive by construction, ensuring that -\mathbf{W} is a Metzler matrix (non-negative off-diagonal entries), which is the standard setting for M-matrix theory. A nonsingular M-matrix requires all principal minors of \mathbf{W} to be positive, which in particular requires W_{ii} > 0 for all i.

Theorem 5.1 (Mean-Square Network Invariance). If \mathbf{W} is a nonsingular M-matrix, then there exists a positive vector \mathbf{p} = [p_1,\dots,p_N]^\top > \mathbf{0} such that the composite Lyapunov function

V(X) = \sum_{i=1}^N p_i V_i(X_i)

satisfies

\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{L} V(X_t)] \leq -\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{W}) \mathbb{E}[V(X_t)],

guaranteeing exponential stability in mean square:

\mathbb{E}[V(X_t)] \leq \mathbb{E}[V(X_0)] e^{-\lambda t},

where \lambda = \lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{W}) > 0.

Proof. By Assumption 5.1, the local generators satisfy

\mathcal{L} V_i \leq -\left(\alpha_i - \frac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2\right) V_i + \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| (V_j - V_i).

Rearranging,

\mathcal{L} V_i \leq -\left(\alpha_i - \frac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2 + \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\|\right) V_i + \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| V_j = -\sum_{j=1}^N W_{ij} V_j.

Multiplying by p_i > 0 and summing over i,

\mathcal{L} V = \sum_{i=1}^N p_i \mathcal{L} V_i \leq -\sum_{i,j} p_i W_{ij} V_j.

Since \mathbf{W} is a nonsingular M-matrix, there exists \mathbf{p} > \mathbf{0} such that \mathbf{W}\mathbf{p} > \mathbf{0}. Setting \lambda = \lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{W}), the composite Lyapunov function decays exponentially in mean square. \square

Corollary 5.1.1 (Stochastic Isolation Threshold). If stochastic volatility in jurisdiction k spikes such that

\boxed{
\frac{1}{2}\sigma_k^2 \geq \alpha_k + \sum_{j \neq k} \|\mathbf{C}_{kj}\|,
}

the k-th diagonal entry of \mathbf{W} becomes non-positive, destroying the M-matrix property. The global GCP dynamically activates stochastic quarantine:

\mathbf{C}_{ik} \to \mathbf{0}, \quad \forall i \neq k,

isolating node k. The remaining (N-1) \times (N-1) interaction matrix \mathbf{W}_{\mathcal{M} \setminus \{k\}} is a nonsingular M-matrix, preserving mean-square stability of the compliant sub-network.

Proof. When

W_{kk} = \alpha_k - \frac{1}{2}\sigma_k^2 + \sum_{j \neq k} \|\mathbf{C}_{kj}\| \leq 0,

the M-matrix property is lost because a nonsingular M-matrix requires all diagonal entries to be positive. Setting \mathbf{C}_{ik} = \mathbf{0} for all i \neq k removes the coupling from node k to the other jurisdictions. The remaining interaction matrix \mathbf{W}_{\mathcal{M} \setminus \{k\}} has the same structure as \mathbf{W} but without the k-th row and column. The entries of \mathbf{W}_{\mathcal{M} \setminus \{k\}} are

W_{ij}^{(\setminus k)} = \begin{cases}
\alpha_i - \frac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2 + \sum_{m \neq i, m \neq k} \|\mathbf{C}_{im}\|, & i = j, \\[6pt]
-\|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\|, & i \neq j,
\end{cases}

for i, j \neq k. If \mathbf{W}_{\mathcal{M} \setminus \{k\}} satisfies the M-matrix condition, the compliant sub-network remains mean-square stable. \square

Remark 5.3. The corrected quarantine threshold now depends on the noise intensity \sigma_k through \tfrac{1}{2}\sigma_k^2 \geq \alpha_k + \sum_{j \neq k}\|\mathbf{C}_{kj}\|. Jurisdictions with higher stochastic volatility require earlier quarantine activation. The condition that the remaining sub-matrix is a nonsingular M-matrix is essential and must be verified explicitly.

5.3 Networked Pacing Condition

Definition 5.4 (Networked Sensitivity Ratio). For jurisdiction i, the networked sensitivity ratio is

\eta_i(\mathbf{X}) = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^M \|\boldsymbol{\eta}_{ij}(\mathbf{X})\| \|\dot{z}_j^{\mathrm{adv}}\| + \gamma_i \Delta_{G,i}(\mathbf{X})}{|\dot{z}_i^{\mathrm{home}}|},

where \boldsymbol{\eta}_{ij} is the cross-sensitivity matrix and \gamma_i > 0 is a regularization parameter.

Theorem 5.2 (Networked Pacing Bound). Under the hypotheses of Theorem 5.1, if the networked pacing condition

|\dot{z}_i^{\mathrm{home}}| \geq \sum_{j=1}^M \|\boldsymbol{\eta}_{ij}(\mathbf{X})\| \cdot \|\dot{z}_j^{\mathrm{adv}}\| + \gamma_i \Delta_{G,i}(\mathbf{X})

holds for all i \in \mathcal{M}, then the global viable set \mathcal{S}_\Lambda is strictly forward-invariant in mean square under the coupled stochastic dynamics.

Proof. The proof follows from the vector Lyapunov inequality derived in Theorem 5.1. The networked pacing condition ensures that the local governance gap dynamics are dissipative, and the M-matrix condition guarantees that the coupled system is mean-square stable. \square

