6. Master Stochastic GCP Viability Theorem

Theorem 6.1 (Master Stochastic GCP Viability Theorem — Sufficient Condition). Let the system dynamics be governed by the stochastic extended game

dX_t = \left( F_G(X_t, u_t, e_t) + R(z_t, u_t)\mathbf{e}_K + P(X_t, \Theta_t, u_t^{\mathrm{adv}})\mathbf{e}_K \right) dt + \Sigma(X_t, u_t) dW_t,

with recursive self-improvement operator R(z,u), adversarial pressure P(X,\Theta,u^{\mathrm{adv}}), and legitimacy dynamics \Lambda_{t+1} = L(\Lambda_t, g_t, \theta_t). Let Assumptions 2.1–2.6, 4.1, and 5.1–5.2 hold. Suppose the following conditions are satisfied:

1. (Stochastic RSI Boundedness)

u_t \in U_{\mathrm{RSI}}^{\mathrm{stoch}}(X_t) \quad \forall t \geq 0.

2. (Stochastic Sensitivity-Ratio Pacing)

|\dot{z}^{\mathrm{home}}| \geq \eta(X_t) |\dot{z}^{\mathrm{adv}}| \quad \forall t \geq 0.

3. (Per-Event Legitimacy Invariant) For every governance event g_t:

\Lambda_{t+1} \geq \Lambda_t - \delta^- \quad \text{on erosive events } g_t \in \mathcal{G}^-,

\Lambda_{t+1} \geq \min(1, \Lambda_t + \delta^+) \quad \text{on restorative events } g_t \in \mathcal{G}^+,

and \Lambda_t \geq \Lambda_{\min} for all t \geq 0.

4. (Epistemic Information Bound)

\delta_I(t) \leq \delta_{I,\max}(t) \quad \forall t \geq 0,

where \delta_{I,\max}(t) = (-\Delta_G(\hat{X}_t))_+ / L_{\mathrm{eff}}.

5. (Mean-Square Network Stability) The corrected interaction matrix \mathbf{W} defined in Definition 5.3 is a nonsingular M-matrix.

Then the extended governable set \mathcal{S}_\Lambda is robustly viable in the almost-sure sense: there exists a control policy \pi: \mathcal{S}_\Lambda \to U such that for all admissible adversarial strategies \pi^{\mathrm{adv}},

\Delta_G(X_t) \leq 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \Lambda_t \geq \Lambda_{\min}

for all t \geq 0, with probability 1.

Proof. The proof combines the almost-sure invariance condition of Theorem 3.1, the stochastic RSI viability condition of Theorem 3.3, the stochastic pacing bound of Theorem 5.2, the per-event legitimacy invariant of Assumption (3), the information threshold of Theorem 4.3, and the mean-square network stability of Theorem 5.1.

Condition (1) ensures that the recursive self-improvement term R remains within the stochastic viability envelope, so the extended dynamics do not exit \mathcal{S}_\Lambda through the RSI channel. Condition (2) ensures that the adversarial pressure P does not outpace the home capability evolution, so the governance gap does not diverge through the competition channel. Condition (3) ensures that the legitimacy variable remains above \Lambda_{\min} at all times, so the trajectory does not exit \mathcal{S}_\Lambda through the legitimacy channel. Condition (4) ensures that the estimation error remains below the state-dependent allowable margin, so the governor's control decisions are based on a sufficiently accurate estimate of the true state. Condition (5) ensures that the multi-jurisdictional coupling does not destabilize the global system, so the trajectory does not exit \mathcal{S}_\Lambda through the network channel.

The five conditions jointly cover the four failure channels — RSI escape, adversarial outrun, legitimacy erosion, and network destabilization — and the epistemic channel. Under the almost-sure invariance conditions of Theorem 3.1, the trajectory remains in \mathcal{S}_\Lambda with probability 1. \square

Remark 6.1 (Sufficiency, Not Necessity). The theorem is stated as a sufficient condition under explicit assumptions rather than as an equivalence. The necessity of conditions (1), (2), (4), and (5) is established by Theorems 3.3, 5.2, 4.3, and 5.1 respectively. The necessity of condition (3) is not established, because the comparison inequalities of Definition A.1 provide one-sided bounds only. A single erosive event with large \delta^- can force \Lambda < \Lambda_{\min} immediately, regardless of the cumulative sum of restorative and erosive events. The per-event invariant is a sufficient condition for legitimacy maintenance but is not necessary. A more precise statement would downgrade the theorem to a sufficient condition under the additional assumption that per-event drops are bounded by \delta^- and that restorative events occur with sufficient frequency.

Remark 6.2 (Two Failure Modes). A system can fail governability in exactly two ways:

\text{Governance Failure} = (\mathcal{Q} < 0) \ \vee \ (\Lambda < \Lambda_{\min}).

A system may be technically governable but institutionally illegitimate (\mathcal{Q} \geq 0 \wedge \Lambda < \Lambda_{\min}), or institutionally legitimate but technically ungovernable (\mathcal{Q} < 0 \wedge \Lambda \geq \Lambda_{\min}). Only their intersection is admissible.

