This final consolidation extends the deterministic framework into three domains: stochastic viability, epistemic asymmetry, and stochastic multi-jurisdictional compositionality. The central dynamical equation remains

\boxed{\dot{\Delta}_G = \frac{\partial G_{\mathrm{req}}}{\partial K}(F_K + R + P) - \dot{G}_{\mathrm{eff}},}

now interpreted as the drift component of a stochastic differential equation for the governance gap, with the adversarial pressure variable renamed from G to P to eliminate overloading.

The key new results are:

1. Stochastic Viability Kernel. The deterministic viability kernel is replaced by the stochastic viability kernel, characterized by the second-order Nagumo condition. The theory distinguishes clearly between almost-sure invariance, requiring diffusion boundary cancellation \Sigma(X,u)^\top \nabla g_k(X) = \mathbf{0} and second-order drift sub-admissibility, and the weaker notion of mean viability, which permits balanced boundary excursions.
2. Stochastic Hamilton–Jacobi–Isaacs Equation. The deterministic max–min Bellman equation is replaced by the second-order HJI PDE, whose value function is characterized as a viscosity solution under the Isaacs condition, nondegeneracy, and boundary regularity.
3. State-Dependent Epistemic Information Threshold. The critical estimation error is derived from the posterior Cramér–Rao lower bound as

\delta_{I,\max}(t) = \frac{\bigl(-\Delta_G(\hat{X}_t)\bigr)_+}{L_{\mathrm{eff}}}.

The theorem is stated as a necessary condition on the estimator, not as a control guarantee: governability is impossible if \delta_I(t) > \delta_{I,\max}(t), regardless of control magnitude.

4. Corrected Mean-Square Network Stability. The M-matrix condition is extended to mean-square exponential stability with corrected diagonal entries

W_{ii} = \alpha_i - \tfrac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2 + \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\|.

The stochastic quarantine corollary is reproved under this correction, yielding the isolation threshold \tfrac{1}{2}\sigma_k^2 \geq \alpha_k + \sum_{j \neq k}\|\mathbf{C}_{kj}\|.

5. Master Theorem as Sufficient Condition. The Master Stochastic GCP Viability Theorem is stated as a sufficient condition under explicit assumptions rather than as an equivalence, because the legitimacy comparison inequalities provide one-sided bounds only.

The five-way separation

\text{Intelligence} \neq \text{Capability} \neq \text{Authority} \neq \text{Governance} \neq \text{Legitimacy}

constitutes the conceptual foundation. The hierarchy of the theory is

\boxed{\text{ACP} \ \to \ \text{GCP} \ \to \ \text{Robust Stochastic Game} \ \to \ \text{Multi-Jurisdictional Network}.}

The objective is not to stop intelligence. It is to ensure that

