Appendix A: Corrected Legitimacy Dynamics

A.1 Formal Classification of Governance Events

Definition A.1 (Legitimacy Operations — Corrected). A governance event is:

· Restorative, written g^+ \in \mathcal{G}^+, if for all \Lambda \in (0,1) and all \theta:

L(\Lambda, g^+, \theta) \geq \min(1, \Lambda + \delta^+), \quad \delta^+ > 0.

· Erosive, written g^- \in \mathcal{G}^-, if for all \Lambda \in (0,1) and all \theta:

L(\Lambda, g^-, \theta) \leq \max(0, \Lambda - \delta^-), \quad \delta^- > 0.

· Neutral otherwise.

Remark A.1 (One-Sided Bounds). The inequalities are one-sided: restorative events guarantee a lower bound on the post-event legitimacy, while erosive events guarantee an upper bound. This asymmetry is essential. A restorative event cannot be assumed to increase legitimacy by exactly \delta^+; it may increase it by more. Similarly, an erosive event may decrease legitimacy by less than \delta^-. The cumulative condition derived from these one-sided bounds is therefore sufficient but not necessary.

A.2 Legitimacy Erosion Under Persistent Erosion

Theorem A.1 (Legitimacy Erosion Under Persistent Erosion). Suppose the governance policy uses an erosive event g^- on a set of times of positive density \varphi > 0, and no restorative events occur. Suppose further that L satisfies

L(\Lambda, g^-, \theta) \leq \max(0, \Lambda - \delta^-)

with \delta^- > 0. Then \Lambda_t \to 0 and, if \Lambda_{\min} > 0, the trajectory exits \mathcal{S}_\Lambda in finite time.

Proof. Each erosive event reduces \Lambda by at least \delta^- until saturation at 0. Under persistent erosion with frequency at least \varphi > 0, after n erosive events,

\Lambda_{t_n} \leq \max(0, \Lambda_0 - n\delta^-).

Since \Lambda \in [0,1], the number of events required to reach 0 is at most \lceil \Lambda_0 / \delta^- \rceil. The time required is at most \lceil \Lambda_0 / \delta^- \rceil / \varphi. If \Lambda_{\min} > 0, the trajectory exits \mathcal{S}_\Lambda at the first time t such that \Lambda_t < \Lambda_{\min}, which occurs in finite time. \square

Remark A.2 (Saturation at Zero). The proof uses the saturation \max(0, \Lambda - \delta^-). Once \Lambda = 0, further erosive events leave \Lambda at 0. Exit occurs only if \Lambda_{\min} > 0. If \Lambda_{\min} = 0, the system remains in \mathcal{S}_\Lambda at the boundary but is permanently illegitimate.

A.3 Legitimacy Restoration

Theorem A.2 (Legitimacy Restoration). Suppose the policy applies a restorative event g^+ at time t with

L(\Lambda_t, g^+, \theta) \geq \min(1, \Lambda_t + \delta^+).

Then \Lambda_{t+1} \geq \min(1, \Lambda_t + \delta^+). If \Lambda_t < \Lambda_{\min} and the policy subsequently applies g^+ repeatedly, the system returns to \mathcal{S}_\Lambda in at most

\left\lceil \frac{\Lambda_{\min} - \Lambda_t}{\delta^+} \right\rceil

steps.

Proof. Iterating the lower bound,

\Lambda_{t+n} \geq \min(1, \Lambda_t + n\delta^+).

The smallest n such that \Lambda_t + n\delta^+ \geq \Lambda_{\min} is \lceil (\Lambda_{\min} - \Lambda_t)/\delta^+ \rceil. \square

Remark A.3 (Necessity of Frequency). Restoration requires restorative events to occur with sufficient frequency to overcome erosion. If erosive events occur at frequency \varphi^-  and restorative events at frequency \varphi^+, the net drift of legitimacy is bounded below by

\dot{\Lambda} \geq \varphi^+ \delta^+ - \varphi^- \delta^-.

If \varphi^+ \delta^+ > \varphi^- \delta^-, legitimacy can be maintained in expectation. This is a necessary condition for legitimacy maintenance in the mean, but not a sufficient condition for almost-sure maintenance.

A.4 Two Failure Modes

Corollary A.2.1 (Two Failure Modes). A system can fail governability in exactly two ways:

\boxed{\text{Governance Failure} = (\mathcal{Q} < 0) \ \vee \ (\Lambda < \Lambda_{\min}).}

A system may be:

· Technically governable but institutionally illegitimate: \mathcal{Q} \geq 0 \wedge \Lambda < \Lambda_{\min}.
· Institutionally legitimate but technically ungovernable: \mathcal{Q} < 0 \wedge \Lambda \geq \Lambda_{\min}.

Only their intersection is admissible:

\mathcal{S}_\Lambda = \{X : \mathcal{Q}(X) \geq 0\} \cap \{\Lambda \geq \Lambda_{\min}\}.

Proof. By definition, X \in \mathcal{S}_\Lambda if and only if \mathcal{Q}(X) \geq 0 and \Lambda \geq \Lambda_{\min}. The complement of \mathcal{S}_\Lambda is therefore the union of \{\mathcal{Q} < 0\} and \{\Lambda < \Lambda_{\min}\}. \square

A.5 Correction to the Cumulative Legitimacy Condition

Remark A.4 (Correction to the Previous Draft). The cumulative legitimacy condition

\sum_{\tau=0}^t \mathbf{1}_{\{g_\tau \in \mathcal{G}^+\}} \delta^+ \geq \sum_{\tau=0}^t \mathbf{1}_{\{g_\tau \in \mathcal{G}^-\}} \delta^- - (\Lambda_0 - \Lambda_{\min})

is a sufficient condition for legitimacy maintenance but is not necessary. The one-sided comparison inequalities of Definition A.1 provide a lower bound for restorative events and an upper bound for erosive events. A single erosive event with large \delta^- can force \Lambda < \Lambda_{\min} immediately, regardless of the cumulative sum.

Corrected Statement (Per-Event Invariant). Legitimacy is maintained if and only if for every t,

\Lambda_{t+1} \geq \Lambda_t - \delta^- \quad \text{on } g_t \in \mathcal{G}^-,

\Lambda_{t+1} \geq \min(1, \Lambda_t + \delta^+) \quad \text{on } g_t \in \mathcal{G}^+,

and \Lambda_t \geq \Lambda_{\min} for all t. This per-event invariant is both necessary and sufficient, whereas the cumulative condition is only sufficient.

