Appendix B: Corrected Multi-Jurisdictional Interaction Matrix

B.1 Derivation of the Corrected Diagonal Entries

Assumption B.1 (Local Dissipativity). There exist \alpha_i > 0 and \sigma_i > 0 such that for each jurisdiction i,

\langle \nabla V_i(X_i), f_i(X_i, u_i, a_i) \rangle \leq -\alpha_i V_i(X_i),

\operatorname{Tr}\left( \Sigma_i(X_i, u_i) \Sigma_i(X_i, u_i)^\top \nabla^2 V_i(X_i) \right) \leq \sigma_i^2 V_i(X_i).

Assumption B.2 (Coupling Bound). There exists a constant L_{\mathbf{C}} > 0 such that for all i, j,

\|\mathbf{C}_{ij}(X_j - X_i)\| \leq \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| \cdot \|X_j - X_i\|,

and the coupling enters the generator of V_i through the bound

\left\langle \nabla V_i, \sum_{j \neq i} \mathbf{C}_{ij}(X_j - X_i) \right\rangle \leq \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| \bigl( V_j - V_i \bigr).

Lemma B.1 (Local Generator Inequality). Under Assumptions B.1 and B.2, the infinitesimal generator of V_i satisfies

\mathcal{L} V_i \leq -\left(\alpha_i - \frac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2\right) V_i + \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| (V_j - V_i).

Proof. By the Itô formula applied to V_i(X_t^i),

\mathcal{L} V_i = \langle \nabla V_i, f_i \rangle + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Tr}(\Sigma_i \Sigma_i^\top \nabla^2 V_i) + \left\langle \nabla V_i, \sum_{j \neq i} \mathbf{C}_{ij}(X_j - X_i) \right\rangle.

Applying Assumption B.1 to the first two terms,

\langle \nabla V_i, f_i \rangle + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Tr}(\Sigma_i \Sigma_i^\top \nabla^2 V_i) \leq -\alpha_i V_i + \frac{1}{2} \sigma_i^2 V_i = -\left(\alpha_i - \frac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2\right) V_i.

Applying Assumption B.2 to the coupling term,

\left\langle \nabla V_i, \sum_{j \neq i} \mathbf{C}_{ij}(X_j - X_i) \right\rangle \leq \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| (V_j - V_i).

Combining,

\mathcal{L} V_i \leq -\left(\alpha_i - \frac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2\right) V_i + \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| (V_j - V_i). \quad \square

Lemma B.2 (Rearrangement). The inequality of Lemma B.1 can be written as

\mathcal{L} V_i \leq -\left(\alpha_i - \frac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2 + \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\|\right) V_i + \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| V_j.

Proof. Expanding the coupling term,

\sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| (V_j - V_i) = \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| V_j - \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| V_i.

Substituting into Lemma B.1,

\mathcal{L} V_i \leq -\left(\alpha_i - \frac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2\right) V_i + \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| V_j - \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| V_i.

Grouping the V_i terms,

\mathcal{L} V_i \leq -\left(\alpha_i - \frac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2 + \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\|\right) V_i + \sum_{j \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\| V_j. \quad \square

Definition B.1 (Corrected Stochastic Interaction Matrix). The systemic interaction matrix \mathbf{W} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N} has entries

\boxed{
W_{ij} = \begin{cases}
\alpha_i - \frac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2 + \sum_{k \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ik}\|, & \text{if } i = j, \\[6pt]
-\|\mathbf{C}_{ij}\|, & \text{if } i \neq j.
\end{cases}
}

Remark B.1 (Correction to the Previous Draft). The diagonal entry in the previous draft was

W_{ii} = \alpha_i - \frac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2 - \sum_{k \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ik}\|,

which subtracts the coupling twice. The correct form, derived from Lemma B.2, adds the coupling terms to the diagonal. The coupling contributes +\sum_{k \neq i} \|\mathbf{C}_{ik}\| to the dissipation of V_i, because the coupling removes V_i from jurisdiction i and redistributes it to neighbors.

Remark B.2 (Metzler Structure). The off-diagonal entries are non-positive by construction, ensuring that -\mathbf{W} is a Metzler matrix (non-negative off-diagonal entries). A nonsingular M-matrix requires all principal minors of \mathbf{W} to be positive, which in particular requires W_{ii} > 0 for all i.

B.2 Mean-Square Network Stability

Theorem B.1 (Mean-Square Network Invariance). If \mathbf{W} is a nonsingular M-matrix, then there exists a positive vector \mathbf{p} = [p_1,\dots,p_N]^\top > \mathbf{0} such that the composite Lyapunov function

V(X) = \sum_{i=1}^N p_i V_i(X_i)

satisfies

\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{L} V(X_t)] \leq -\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{W}) \mathbb{E}[V(X_t)],

guaranteeing exponential stability in mean square:

\mathbb{E}[V(X_t)] \leq \mathbb{E}[V(X_0)] e^{-\lambda t},

where \lambda = \lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{W}) > 0.

Proof. By Lemma B.2,

\mathcal{L} V_i \leq -\sum_{j=1}^N W_{ij} V_j.

Multiplying by p_i > 0 and summing over i,

\mathcal{L} V = \sum_{i=1}^N p_i \mathcal{L} V_i \leq -\sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{j=1}^N p_i W_{ij} V_j = -\sum_{j=1}^N \left(\sum_{i=1}^N p_i W_{ij}\right) V_j.

Let \mathbf{q} = \mathbf{W}^\top \mathbf{p}. Then

\mathcal{L} V \leq -\sum_{j=1}^N q_j V_j.

Since \mathbf{W} is a nonsingular M-matrix, there exists \mathbf{p} > \mathbf{0} such that \mathbf{W}\mathbf{p} > \mathbf{0} (equivalently, \mathbf{W}^\top \mathbf{p} > \mathbf{0}). Let \lambda = \lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{W}) > 0. Then

\mathcal{L} V \leq -\lambda \sum_{j=1}^N p_j V_j = -\lambda V.

Taking expectations and applying Gronwall's inequality,

\mathbb{E}[V(X_t)] \leq \mathbb{E}[V(X_0)] e^{-\lambda t}. \quad \square

B.3 Stochastic Quarantine Corollary

