\mathbf{C}_{ik} \to \mathbf{0}, \quad \forall i \neq k,

isolating node k. The remaining (N-1) \times (N-1) interaction matrix \mathbf{W}_{\mathcal{M} \setminus \{k\}} is a nonsingular M-matrix, preserving mean-square stability of the compliant sub-network.

Proof. When

W_{kk} = \alpha_k - \frac{1}{2}\sigma_k^2 + \sum_{j \neq k} \|\mathbf{C}_{kj}\| \leq 0,

the M-matrix property is lost because a nonsingular M-matrix requires all diagonal entries to be positive. Setting \mathbf{C}_{ik} = \mathbf{0} for all i \neq k removes the coupling from node k to the other jurisdictions. The remaining interaction matrix \mathbf{W}_{\mathcal{M} \setminus \{k\}} has the same structure as \mathbf{W} but without the k-th row and column. The entries of \mathbf{W}_{\mathcal{M} \setminus \{k\}} are

W_{ij}^{(\setminus k)} = \begin{cases}
\alpha_i - \frac{1}{2}\sigma_i^2 + \sum_{m \neq i, m \neq k} \|\mathbf{C}_{im}\|, & i = j, \\[6pt]
