Appendix C: Stochastic Viability — Detailed Proofs

C.1 Itô Formula for Boundary Functions

Lemma C.1 (Itô Formula for g_k). For a C^2 function g_k: \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R} and the Itô process

dX_t = f(X_t, u_t, a_t)\,dt + \Sigma(X_t, u_t)\,dW_t,

the differential of g_k(X_t) is

dg_k(X_t) = \left( \langle \nabla g_k, f \rangle + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Tr}(\Sigma \Sigma^\top \nabla^2 g_k) \right) dt + \langle \nabla g_k, \Sigma \, dW_t \rangle.

Proof. This is the standard Itô formula for a C^2 function of an Itô process. The drift term is

\langle \nabla g_k, f \rangle + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Tr}(\Sigma \Sigma^\top \nabla^2 g_k),

and the diffusion term is \langle \nabla g_k, \Sigma \, dW_t \rangle. \square

C.2 Second-Order Nagumo Condition

Theorem C.1 (Stochastic Invariance / Second-Order Nagumo Condition). The domain \mathcal{S}_\Lambda is almost surely forward-invariant under

dX_t = f(X_t, u_t, a_t)\,dt + \Sigma(X_t, u_t)\,dW_t

if for every boundary point X \in \partial \mathcal{S}_\Lambda and every active constraint k \in I^*(X), there exists a control u \in \mathcal{U} such that for all a \in \mathcal{A}:

1. (Diffusion boundary cancellation)

\boxed{\Sigma(X,u)^\top \nabla g_k(X) = \mathbf{0}_{q \times 1}.}

2. (Second-order drift sub-admissibility)

\boxed{\langle \nabla g_k(X), f(X,u,a) \rangle + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Tr}\left( \Sigma(X,u)\Sigma(X,u)^\top \nabla^2 g_k(X) \right) \leq 0.}

Proof. Applying Lemma C.1 to the active scalar boundary function g_k(X_t):

dg_k(X_t) = \left( \langle \nabla g_k, f \rangle + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Tr}(\Sigma\Sigma^\top \nabla^2 g_k) \right) dt + \langle \nabla g_k, \Sigma \, dW_t \rangle.

For g_k(X_t) \leq 0 to hold almost surely without stochastic excursions across the boundary, the stochastic differential term \langle \nabla g_k, \Sigma \, dW_t \rangle must vanish identically on g_k(X) = 0. This requires

\Sigma(X,u)^\top \nabla g_k(X) = \mathbf{0}.

With the diffusion term eliminated, the drift condition reduces to

\langle \nabla g_k, f \rangle + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Tr}(\Sigma\Sigma^\top \nabla^2 g_k) \leq 0,

which guarantees dg_k(X_t) \leq 0 on \partial \mathcal{S}_\Lambda. \square

C.3 Mean Viability

Theorem C.2 (Mean Viability Condition). Under Assumptions 2.1–2.6, the domain \mathcal{S}_\Lambda is mean-viable under the controlled stochastic dynamics if and only if there exists a policy \pi such that for every active constraint k \in I^*(X),

\mathbb{E}\left[ \langle \nabla g_k, f \rangle + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Tr}(\Sigma\Sigma^\top \nabla^2 g_k) \right] \leq 0

on the boundary \partial \mathcal{S}_\Lambda.

Proof. Necessity: if the domain is mean-viable, then \mathbb{E}[g_k(X_t)] \leq 0 for all t. Taking the time derivative at t = 0 on the boundary,

\frac{d}{dt} \mathbb{E}[g_k(X_t)] \bigg|_{t=0} = \mathbb{E}\left[ \langle \nabla g_k, f \rangle + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Tr}(\Sigma\Sigma^\top \nabla^2 g_k) \right] \leq 0.

Sufficiency: if the drift expectation is non-positive on the boundary, then \mathbb{E}[g_k(X_t)] is non-increasing in expectation, so \mathbb{E}[g_k(X_t)] \leq 0 for all t. \square

Remark C.1 (Comparison). Mean viability is strictly weaker than almost-sure invariance. The almost-sure condition requires \Sigma^\top \nabla g_k = 0, while mean viability permits \Sigma^\top \nabla g_k \neq 0, provided the diffusion term has zero expectation. For applications tolerating occasional boundary excursions compensated by restorative control, mean viability suffices.

C.4 Existence of the Stochastic Viability Kernel

Theorem C.3 (Existence of Stochastic Viability Kernel). Under Assumptions 2.1–2.6, the stochastic viability kernel \mathrm{Viab}_{\mathrm{stoch}}(\mathcal{S}_\Lambda) is non-empty, closed, and is itself viable under the controlled stochastic dynamics.

Proof sketch. Under the Marchaud conditions and the regularity assumptions, the set-valued map X \mapsto F(X,U(X),E) is upper semicontinuous with non-empty compact convex values. The viability kernel is the largest closed viable subset of \mathcal{S}_\Lambda, and its existence follows from the Aubin–Frankowska viability theorem for controlled diffusions. \square

Remark C.2 (Non-emptiness). The non-emptiness of the viability kernel requires that \mathcal{S}_\Lambda contain at least one point from which a viable trajectory exists. If \mathcal{S}_\Lambda is empty or if no control can keep the trajectory in \mathcal{S}_\Lambda, the kernel is empty.

