Appendix D: Epistemic Asymmetry — Detailed Derivations

D.1 Kushner–Stratonovich Equation

Theorem D.1 (Kushner–Stratonovich Equation). Under regularity conditions on f, \Sigma, h, the conditional distribution \pi_t satisfies

d\pi_t(\phi) = \pi_t(\mathcal{L}\phi)\,dt + \pi_t\left(\phi h^\top - \pi_t(\phi)\pi_t(h)^\top\right)(\Gamma\Gamma^\top)^{-1}\left(dY_t - \pi_t(h)\,dt\right),

for all test functions \phi \in C_b^2(\mathbb{R}^d), where \mathcal{L} is the infinitesimal generator of the signal process X_t.

Proof sketch. The result is the standard nonlinear filtering equation for partially observed systems. The derivation proceeds via the reference probability measure approach: the unnormalized filtering process satisfies the Zakai equation, and the Kushner–Stratonovich equation is obtained by normalization. \square

D.2 Posterior Cramér–Rao Lower Bound

Theorem D.2 (Posterior Cramér–Rao Lower Bound). For any unbiased estimator \hat{X}_t of X_t based on \mathcal{F}_t^Y, the mean-square error satisfies

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|\hat{X}_t - X_t\|^2 \right] \geq \operatorname{Tr}\left( \mathbf{J}_t^{-1} \right),

where \mathbf{J}_t is the posterior Fisher information matrix.

Proof. The result follows from the van Trees version of the Cramér–Rao inequality for random parameters. The posterior Fisher information matrix \mathbf{J}_t is the inverse of the Cramér–Rao bound for the mean-square error matrix. \square

Remark D.1 (Interpretation). The bound is a lower bound on the expected squared error. It does not assert the existence of an almost-sure bound on \delta_I(t) = \|\hat{X}_t - X_t\|, nor does it guarantee that any particular estimator attains the bound.

D.3 State-Dependent Critical Information Threshold

Assumption D.1 (Bounded Estimation Error). There exists a deterministic function \delta_{I,\max}: [0,\infty) \to (0,\infty) such that for all t \geq 0, \delta_I(t) \leq \delta_{I,\max}(t) with probability 1. This is an assumption on the estimator, not a consequence of the CRLB.

Theorem D.3 (State-Dependent Critical Information Threshold). Suppose the governance control policy \pi is based on the estimate \hat{X}_t rather than the true state X_t. Let L_{\mathrm{eff}} be the Lipschitz constant of G_{\mathrm{eff}}. Define the state-dependent allowable error

\delta_{I,\max}(t) = \frac{\bigl(-\Delta_G(\hat{X}_t)\bigr)_+}{L_{\mathrm{eff}}},

where (\cdot)_+ = \max(\cdot, 0). If \delta_I(t) > \delta_{I,\max}(t), then

\Delta_G(X_t) > 0

for all admissible control policies u \in \mathcal{U}, regardless of the control magnitude \|G_{\mathrm{eff}}\|.

Proof. The governance gap is a function of the true state:

\Delta_G(X_t) = G_{\mathrm{req}}(K_t) - G_{\mathrm{eff}}(X_t).

When the governor uses the estimate \hat{X}_t, the effective governance effectiveness is G_{\mathrm{eff}}(\hat{X}_t). The true governance gap satisfies

\Delta_G(X_t) = \Delta_G(\hat{X}_t) + \left( G_{\mathrm{eff}}(\hat{X}_t) - G_{\mathrm{eff}}(X_t) \right).

The estimation error term satisfies

\|G_{\mathrm{eff}}(\hat{X}_t) - G_{\mathrm{eff}}(X_t)\| \leq L_{\mathrm{eff}} \|\hat{X}_t - X_t\| = L_{\mathrm{eff}} \delta_I(t),

where L_{\mathrm{eff}} is the Lipschitz constant of G_{\mathrm{eff}}. For the true governance gap to remain non-positive, we require

\Delta_G(\hat{X}_t) + L_{\mathrm{eff}} \delta_I(t) \leq 0.

When \Delta_G(\hat{X}_t) < 0, this is equivalent to

\delta_I(t) \leq \frac{-\Delta_G(\hat{X}_t)}{L_{\mathrm{eff}}} = \delta_{I,\max}(t).

When \Delta_G(\hat{X}_t) \geq 0, the allowable error is \delta_{I,\max}(t) = 0, and any positive estimation error causes \Delta_G(X_t) > 0. Therefore, if \delta_I(t) > \delta_{I,\max}(t), the true governance gap satisfies \Delta_G(X_t) > 0 regardless of the control policy. \square

Remark D.2 (Necessity, Not Sufficiency). The threshold is a necessary condition on the estimator: if the estimation error exceeds \delta_{I,\max}(t), governability is lost. It is not a sufficient condition: even if \delta_I(t) \leq \delta_{I,\max}(t), the control policy must still be chosen to keep the system in \mathcal{S}_\Lambda.

Remark D.3 (State-Dependence). The bound is state-dependent and decreases as the system approaches the boundary of the governable set. When \Delta_G(\hat{X}_t) \to 0^-, the allowable error \delta_{I,\max}(t) \to 0. This captures the intuition that near the boundary, even small estimation errors can push the system into the ungovernable region.

D.4 Information–Control Tradeoff

Corollary D.3.1 (Information–Control Tradeoff). The critical information threshold satisfies the lower bound

\delta_{I,\max}(t) \geq \sqrt{\operatorname{Tr}\left( \mathbf{J}_t^{-1} \right)}

only in the following negative sense: if

\delta_{I,\max}(t) < \sqrt{\mathbb{E}\operatorname{Tr}\left( \mathbf{J}_t^{-1} \right)},

then no estimator can guarantee \delta_I(t) \leq \delta_{I,\max}(t) for all t with probability 1. Consequently, the governor must maintain a minimum level of information quality, characterized by the Fisher information, to preserve governability with high probability.

Proof. By Theorem D.2, the MMSE estimation error satisfies \mathbb{E}[\delta_I^2] \geq \operatorname{Tr}(\mathbf{J}_t^{-1}). If \delta_{I,\max}(t) < \sqrt{\operatorname{Tr}(\mathbf{J}_t^{-1})}, then there exists a positive probability that \delta_I(t) > \delta_{I,\max}(t), and governability is lost. \square

Remark D.4 (Correction to the Previous Draft). The previous draft's Corollary 4.3.1 asserted a positive lower bound on \delta_{I,\max} in terms of the Fisher information. This was an overreach: the CRLB bounds the expected squared error, not the almost-sure error. The corrected statement is a negative result: no estimator can achieve an almost-sure error bound below the square root of the CRLB in expectation.

Remark D.5 (Design Implication). The corollary implies that the governor must invest in sensing and estimation to maintain the Fisher information above a threshold. If the Fisher information drops (e.g., due to sensor degradation or increased observation noise), the allowable estimation error shrinks, and the governor must either reduce the estimation error (by improving the estimator) or increase the governance margin (by reducing G_{\mathrm{req}} or increasing G_{\mathrm{eff}}).

